/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ai-200.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ai-200", [["az1",0,1,"A team builds a Python API image on every commit. Developers do not have Docker installed, and the build must run in Azure. Which command should the pipeline run?",["az acr build --registry myreg --image api:{{.Run.ID}} .","docker build -t myreg.azurecr.io/api .","az acr import --name myreg --source api:latest","az containerapp up --source . --ingress internal"],0,"az acr build is an ACR quick task: it uploads the context and builds and pushes the image in Azure without a local Docker engine. docker build needs local Docker, and az acr import copies an existing image rather than building one.","Skills 1: ACR",[null,"docker build runs on a local Docker engine, which the developers do not have, and it does not push the image to ACR.","az acr import copies an image that already exists in another registry; it does not build anything from source.","az containerapp up builds and deploys a Container App in one step; it is not the registry build step the pipeline needs, and internal ingress is irrelevant."],null],["az2",0,1,"A company wants all its container images rebuilt automatically when Microsoft publishes a patched Python base image. What should the developer configure?",["A Container Apps scale rule on image age","An ACR task with a base image update trigger","A retention policy for untagged manifests","Geo-replication of the registry to a second Azure region"],1,"ACR Tasks can track the base image in a Dockerfile and rebuild the app image when that base image is updated. Retention policies and geo-replication do not rebuild anything.","Skills 1: ACR Tasks",["Container Apps scale rules change replica counts based on load or events; there is no rule on image age and scaling never rebuilds images.",null,"A retention policy deletes untagged manifests after a number of days; it cleans up storage but never triggers a rebuild.","Geo-replication copies the registry's content to other regions for faster pulls; it does not watch base images or rebuild."],null],["az3",0,1,"A registry must be reachable only through a private endpoint in a virtual network and replicated to a second region. Which ACR tier is required?",["Basic","Standard","Premium","Any tier with admin user enabled"],2,"Private endpoints and geo-replication are Premium features. Basic and Standard differ in storage and throughput but do not offer these.","Skills 1: ACR",["Basic is the entry tier for development with lower storage and throughput; it supports neither private endpoints nor geo-replication.","Standard adds storage and throughput over Basic but still lacks private endpoints and geo-replication.",null,"The admin user is an authentication option available on every tier; it has nothing to do with private endpoints or replication."],null],["az4",0,1,"A Container App must pull images from ACR without any stored password. What is the recommended setup?",["Enable the ACR admin user and store its password as a Container Apps secret","Make the registry repository public for the app's IP range","Create a service principal and paste its secret into an environment variable","Give the app's managed identity the AcrPull role on the registry"],3,"A managed identity with AcrPull removes stored secrets and grants only pull rights. The admin user and service principal both leave a secret to store and rotate.","Skills 1: ACR authentication",["The admin user is one shared, full-rights username and password; storing it as a secret still leaves a credential to protect and rotate.","ACR repositories cannot be made public for an IP range, and anonymous pull would drop authentication rather than use an identity.","A service principal secret in an environment variable is still a stored password that can leak and must be rotated.",null],null],["az5",0,1,"Operations wants production to run exactly the image that passed testing, even if someone later pushes a new image with the same tag. How should the image be referenced?",["By the latest tag","By its manifest digest","By the repository name only","By a tag that includes the registry name"],1,"A digest (sha256:...) identifies one immutable manifest. Tags, including latest, can be moved to point at a different image.","Skills 1: Tags and digests",["latest is a movable tag that any later push can repoint, which is exactly the risk described.",null,"A repository name alone resolves to the latest tag by default, so it gives no guarantee about which image runs.","Adding the registry name only makes the reference fully qualified; the tag part can still be moved to a new image."],null],["az6",0,1,"A custom Linux container on App Service listens on port 8000, and the site returns errors because the platform cannot reach the app. Which app setting fixes this?",["WEBSITES_PORT=8000","WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE=1","SCM_DO_BUILD_DURING_DEPLOYMENT=true","FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME=python"],0,"WEBSITES_PORT tells App Service which container port to forward traffic to. The other settings control package deployment, build on deploy and Functions runtime.","Skills 1: App Service containers",[null,"WEBSITE_RUN_FROM_PACKAGE mounts a zip package as the site content; it is for code deployments, not for container port mapping.","SCM_DO_BUILD_DURING_DEPLOYMENT tells the Kudu/Oryx build to run on code deploy; it does not tell App Service which port the container uses.","FUNCTIONS_WORKER_RUNTIME selects the language worker for Azure Functions and has no effect on a custom container web app's port."],null],["az7",0,1,"A team wants to send 10% of users to a new version of a Container App while 90% stay on the current one. What must they configure?",["Single revision mode with a new image tag","A KEDA HTTP scale rule with 10 concurrent requests","Multiple revision mode with traffic weights on each revision","Two Container Apps environments behind one shared external ingress endpoint"],2,"Traffic splitting between revisions needs multiple revision mode, where each active revision gets a percentage. Single revision mode moves all traffic to the newest revision.","Skills 1: Container Apps revisions",["Single revision mode moves all traffic to the newest revision once it is ready, so a 90/10 split is not possible.","A KEDA HTTP scale rule changes the number of replicas based on concurrent requests; it does not divide users between versions.",null,"Two environments would be two separate network boundaries; Container Apps does not share one ingress endpoint across environments for weighted splitting."],null],["az8",0,1,"A background worker in Container Apps reads a Service Bus queue. It should run zero replicas when the queue is empty and add replicas as the backlog grows. What should be configured?",["An HTTP scale rule with min replicas 1 and max replicas 10 on the worker","A custom azure-servicebus scale rule with min replicas 0","A CPU scale rule with min replicas 0","A fixed replica count of 5"],1,"The KEDA azure-servicebus scaler scales on queue length and can scale to zero when min replicas is 0. CPU rules cannot scale to zero, and HTTP rules only react to incoming requests.","Skills 1: Container Apps scaling",["An HTTP rule reacts to incoming requests, which a queue worker does not receive, and min replicas 1 prevents scaling to zero.",null,"CPU and memory scale rules cannot scale an app to zero, because they need a running replica to measure usage.","A fixed count of 5 replicas never reacts to the backlog and keeps billing even when the queue is empty."],null],["az9",0,1,"An internal API in Container Apps must be callable only by other apps in the same environment, not from the internet. How should ingress be set?",["Disable ingress and use Dapr only","External ingress with IP restrictions","Internal ingress","External ingress on port 443 with a custom domain"],2,"Internal ingress exposes the app only inside the Container Apps environment (and its virtual network). External ingress is reachable from the internet even with extra restrictions.","Skills 1: Container Apps ingress",["Disabling ingress removes the HTTP endpoint entirely, so other apps could not call the API over HTTP; Dapr is optional and not required for this.","External ingress is still published to the internet; IP restrictions only filter callers and do not make the app internal.",null,"External ingress with a custom domain on 443 is explicitly internet-facing, the opposite of the requirement."],null],["az10",0,1,"A nightly batch step should run in a container at 02:00, finish, and stop billing until the next run. Which Container Apps feature fits best?",["A scheduled Container Apps job","A Container App with min replicas 1","An App Service WebJob in a custom container","A second revision with 0% traffic"],0,"Container Apps jobs run to completion; a scheduled job uses a cron expression and does not keep a replica running between runs. A Container App with min replicas 1 bills all day.","Skills 1: Container Apps jobs",[null,"A Container App with min replicas 1 keeps a replica running (and billed) all day even when there is no work.","WebJobs run inside an App Service plan that is billed continuously, and they are not the Container Apps feature for run-to-completion tasks.","A revision with 0% traffic still exists as a running app revision; traffic weight does not schedule work or stop billing."],null],["az11",0,1,"An AKS Deployment's pods are stuck with ErrImagePull when pulling from a private ACR. What is the simplest fix?",["Add a ConfigMap with the registry URL","Increase the pod memory limit","Change the Service type to LoadBalancer","Run az aks update --attach-acr"],3,"--attach-acr gives the cluster's kubelet identity the AcrPull role on the registry. ConfigMaps, memory limits and Service types do not affect image pulls.","Skills 1: AKS",["A ConfigMap only passes configuration to pods; the registry URL is already in the image name and the problem is missing pull permission.","Memory limits affect running containers, but ErrImagePull happens before the container starts, when the image cannot be pulled.","The Service type controls how traffic reaches pods; it has no effect on whether nodes can pull the image.",null],null],["az12",0,1,"A Kubernetes Deployment manifest should keep three copies of an API pod running. Which field sets this?",["spec.replicas","spec.template.spec.containers.count","metadata.labels.replicas","spec.strategy.maxSurge"],0,"spec.replicas sets the desired number of pods. maxSurge only controls how many extra pods may be created during a rolling update.","Skills 1: AKS manifests",[null,"containers is a list of container definitions in the pod template; there is no count field and it does not control how many pods run.","Labels are key/value metadata used for selection; a label named replicas has no effect on the pod count.","maxSurge limits how many extra pods can be created during a rolling update; it does not set the steady-state count."],null],["az13",0,1,"A Python image is 1.2 GB because it includes compilers used only to build wheels. How can the developer shrink the final image?",["Add more layers with RUN rm commands at the end","Use a multi-stage build with a slim final image","Change the tag from latest to slim","Enable ACR geo-replication"],1,"A multi-stage build keeps build tools in an earlier stage and copies only the output into a small final image. Deleting files in a later layer does not shrink earlier layers.","Skills 1: Dockerfiles",["Files deleted in a later layer still exist in the earlier layers, so the image does not get smaller.",null,"Changing the tag to slim only changes the base image; the compilers installed during the build would still end up in the image.","Geo-replication copies the image to more regions; it does not change the image size and adds storage."],null],["az14",0,1,"Which statement about Container Apps secrets is correct?",["Secrets are stored in the container image and read at build time","Secrets can only be plain text typed into the portal","Secrets are shared automatically with every app in the environment","Secrets belong to one app and can reference Key Vault"],3,"Container Apps secrets belong to one app and can be Key Vault references resolved with a managed identity. They are not baked into images or shared across apps.","Skills 1: Container Apps secrets",["Container Apps secrets are app-level settings injected at runtime; they are never baked into the container image.","Secrets can be plain values or Key Vault references resolved with a managed identity, so they are not limited to portal-typed text.","Secrets are scoped to a single Container App and are not shared automatically with other apps in the environment.",null],null],["az15",0,1,"A web app on App Service runs a custom container. The team wants to test a new image in production conditions and swap it live with no downtime. What should they use?",["A deployment slot, then swap","A second App Service plan","Scale out to two instances","A new ACR webhook on the production slot"],0,"Deployment slots let you deploy and warm up a new version, then swap it with production. Scaling out or adding a plan does not give a staged swap.","Skills 1: App Service containers",[null,"A second App Service plan is just more compute; it has no built-in staging or swap between versions.","Scaling out adds instances of the same version; it does not let you stage and swap a new image.","An ACR webhook can trigger continuous deployment, but on the production slot it would push the new image live without a staged test and swap."],null],["az16",0,1,"Which statement correctly describes Azure Container Apps environments?",["Each app must be in its own environment","Environments are Kubernetes clusters you manage with kubectl","Apps in an environment share networking and logging","Environments exist only for Container Apps jobs"],2,"An environment is a secure boundary around a group of apps that share networking and Log Analytics settings. Microsoft manages the underlying Kubernetes; you do not use kubectl on it.","Skills 1: Container Apps",["Many apps can share one environment; putting each app in its own environment is optional, not required.","The underlying Kubernetes is managed by Microsoft and hidden; you cannot use kubectl against a Container Apps environment.",null,"Environments host both Container Apps and Container Apps jobs; they are not limited to jobs."],null],["az17",0,1,"An ACR repository holds thousands of old image versions, and storage costs keep rising. What should the developer set up?",["Geo-replication to spread storage across regions","A scheduled acr purge task with an age filter","Content trust on the repository","A new registry for each release"],1,"acr purge in a scheduled task deletes tags and manifests older than a set age that match a filter. Geo-replication adds copies and raises storage.","Skills 1: ACR",["Geo-replication adds copies of every image to more regions, which increases storage instead of reducing it.",null,"Content trust signs images so clients can verify them; it does not delete old versions or reduce storage.","A new registry per release multiplies registries to manage and still keeps every old image unless something deletes them."],null],["az18",0,1,"In a Kubernetes Deployment, what is the effect of setting resources.requests for CPU and memory?",["The scheduler places the pod only on a node with that much unreserved capacity","The container is killed as soon as its usage reaches that amount of CPU or memory","The pod gets a public IP address","The image is pulled only once per node"],0,"Requests are what the scheduler reserves when placing a pod. Limits, not requests, cap usage (a container over its memory limit is killed).","Skills 1: AKS manifests",[null,"Killing a container over its memory usage is the effect of limits, not requests; CPU over a limit is throttled rather than killed.","Public IPs come from Services such as LoadBalancer, not from resource requests on the pod.","Image pull behavior is controlled by imagePullPolicy and node caching, not by resource requests."],null],["az19",0,1,"A developer wants to run a new Container App revision for testing through its own URL without sending it any production traffic. What can they use?",["An internal ingress on a second environment","A KEDA rule with min replicas 0","Single revision mode with a new suffix","A revision label with 0% traffic weight"],3,"In multiple revision mode, a label gives a revision its own stable URL, and a 0% weight keeps it out of the main traffic split. Single revision mode would move all traffic to it.","Skills 1: Container Apps revisions",["A second environment would be a separate deployment with its own network, not a test URL for one revision of the same app.","A KEDA rule with min replicas 0 only controls scaling; it gives the revision no separate URL.","In single revision mode the new revision receives all traffic as soon as it is ready, which is the opposite of the goal.",null],null],["az20",0,1,"Why should a container run as a non-root user?",["It makes the image build faster","It is required for ACR to accept the push","It limits damage if an attacker breaks into the process","It lets the container listen on port 80 without extra settings"],2,"A non-root user reduces what an attacker can do after compromising the app. It is not required by ACR, and binding to ports below 1024 is actually harder without root.","Skills 1: Dockerfiles",["The user a container runs as has no effect on how quickly the image layers are built.","ACR accepts images regardless of the user they run as; running as non-root is a runtime hardening practice, not a push requirement.",null,"It is the opposite: binding to ports below 1024 normally needs root or an extra capability, so non-root apps usually listen on a high port."],null],["az21",0,1,"A team deploys the same image to Container Apps in three regions and wants pulls to stay in-region for speed. What should they use?",["Three Basic registries kept in sync with a nightly copy script","A Premium registry with geo-replication to each region","A Standard registry with a CDN in front","An ACR token per region"],1,"Geo-replication on Premium keeps one registry name while serving pulls from a nearby replica. Separate registries need manual syncing, and ACR does not sit behind a CDN.","Skills 1: ACR",["Separate Basic registries need custom sync scripts, have different names per region and can drift out of date.",null,"ACR cannot be placed behind a CDN for image pulls, and Standard has no geo-replication.","ACR tokens are scoped credentials for repository access; they do not change where images are served from."],null],["az22",0,2,"An app reads a user profile by id many times per second, and the container is partitioned by /userId. Which approach costs the fewest RUs?",["A query with SELECT * FROM c WHERE c.id = @id AND c.userId = @uid","A cross-partition query with ORDER BY","A point read using read_item(item=id, partition_key=userId)","A change feed read filtered by id"],2,"A point read with the id and partition key is the cheapest operation, about 1 RU for a 1 KB item. A query for the same item costs more because the engine must process it.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB SDK",["A query for one item still goes through the query engine and costs more RUs than a point read, even with the partition key.","A cross-partition query with ORDER BY fans out to every partition and is one of the most expensive ways to fetch a single item.",null,"The change feed lists changes over time; it is not a lookup mechanism and reading it to find one item wastes RUs."],null],["az23",0,2,"A multi-tenant SaaS app stores documents in Cosmos DB. Most queries filter by tenantId, and some tenants are far larger than others. Which partition key choice avoids hot partitions while keeping queries targeted?",["A hierarchical partition key such as /tenantId then /userId","/country, because it has few values","/createdDate, because it grows over time","/type, because it cleanly separates the different kinds of documents"],0,"A hierarchical key keeps tenant queries targeted while spreading large tenants across more partitions. Low-cardinality or date keys concentrate writes on a few partitions.","Skills 2: Partitioning",[null,"/country has very few distinct values, so a few large countries would create hot partitions.","A date key sends all of today's writes to the same partition, creating a hot partition for writes.","/type has only a handful of values, so partitions would be huge and uneven and tenant queries would fan out."],null],["az24",0,2,"Each item in a Cosmos DB container stores a 1,536-dimension embedding at /embedding. Write RU cost is high. What should the developer change?",["Switch the account to Strong consistency","Exclude /embedding/* from the range index and use a vector index for that path","Remove the partition key","Store the embedding as a string instead"],1,"Indexing every number of an embedding in the normal index wastes RUs; the path should be excluded and covered by a vector index instead. Strong consistency adds cost rather than reducing it.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",["Strong consistency changes read guarantees and raises read cost; it does nothing to reduce indexing cost on writes.",null,"Every container must have a partition key; removing it is not possible and would not reduce indexing cost.","Storing the embedding as a string would break vector search and still gets indexed by the range index."],null],["az25",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB for NoSQL query returns the five items most similar to a query vector @v?",["SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c ORDER BY VectorDistance(c.embedding, @v)","SELECT c.id FROM c WHERE c.embedding = @v LIMIT 5","SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c WHERE ARRAY_CONTAINS(c.embedding, @v, true)","SELECT TOP 5 c.id FROM c ORDER BY c.embedding DESC"],0,"VectorDistance computes similarity using the container's vector policy, and ORDER BY with TOP returns the nearest items. Equality, ARRAY_CONTAINS and plain ORDER BY do not compare vectors by similarity.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",[null,"Equality compares the whole array for an exact match; it does not measure similarity, and Cosmos DB NoSQL uses TOP rather than LIMIT.","ARRAY_CONTAINS checks whether an array contains a value; it does not rank items by vector similarity.","ORDER BY on the embedding array sorts by array values, not by distance to the query vector @v."],null],["az26",0,2,"A developer creates a new container for embeddings. Which setting must be defined in the container vector policy?",["The consistency level","The throughput in RU/s","The TTL for each vector","The embedding path, data type, dimensions and distance function"],3,"The vector policy describes each embedding path's data type, dimensions and distance function. Consistency is set on the account, and throughput and TTL are separate container settings.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",["Consistency is an account-level setting (optionally relaxed per request), not part of the container vector policy.","Throughput is provisioned on the database or container separately and is not part of the vector policy.","TTL is set on the container or per item for the whole document; vectors have no separate TTL in the policy.",null],null],["az27",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB vector index type is designed for large collections and gives fast approximate search with low latency at scale?",["flat","range","diskANN","spatial"],2,"diskANN is the approximate nearest neighbor index built for large vector sets. flat does exact search and suits small sets; range and spatial are not vector indexes.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB vector search",["flat performs exact brute-force search and is limited to 505 dimensions, so it suits small sets rather than large collections.","A range index supports equality and range filters on scalar values; it is not a vector index.",null,"A spatial index supports geospatial queries on GeoJSON points and polygons, not vector similarity search."],null],["az28",0,2,"New product descriptions are written to Cosmos DB. An embedding must be generated for each new or updated item without polling. What should the developer use?",["A timer trigger that queries all items for changes once every minute","An Azure Functions Cosmos DB trigger based on the change feed","A Cosmos DB stored procedure called by the client","Strong consistency with a read-after-write"],1,"The Cosmos DB trigger reads the change feed and runs for each batch of new or changed items. A timer that queries everything is polling and costs far more RUs.","Skills 2: Change feed",["A timer that queries everything each minute is polling, adds latency and burns RUs scanning unchanged items.",null,"Stored procedures run only when a client calls them; they cannot react automatically to new or changed items.","Consistency and read-after-write affect what a client reads, not whether anything runs when data changes."],null],["az29",0,2,"A change feed processor runs on three instances. How do the instances avoid processing the same changes twice?",["Each instance reads a different database","They use Strong consistency","Each instance filters by a random number","They share a lease container that tracks which instance owns each partition range"],3,"The lease container stores ownership and checkpoints for each feed range so work is split and progress survives restarts. Consistency level does not coordinate consumers.","Skills 2: Change feed",["Instances of one processor read the same monitored container; splitting by database is not how work is shared.","Consistency level affects read guarantees; it does not coordinate which consumer handles which partition range.","Random filtering would drop or duplicate changes and gives no checkpointing across restarts.",null],null],["az30",0,2,"A developer notices that deleted items never show up in the change feed in latest version mode. What is a common way to handle deletes?",["Use a soft-delete flag and let TTL remove the item later","Switch to Eventual consistency","Read the change feed from the beginning each time","Use a stored procedure that raises an error whenever an item is deleted"],0,"Latest version mode does not include deletes, so a soft-delete field (an update the feed sees) plus TTL is a standard pattern. All versions and deletes mode is the other option.","Skills 2: Change feed",[null,"Consistency level does not control what the change feed contains; latest version mode omits deletes at every level.","Re-reading from the beginning still shows only current items, so deleted items remain invisible and RU cost rises.","Stored procedures do not run on deletes made through other paths, and raising an error would block deletes rather than capture them."],null],["az31",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB consistency level is the default and gives read-your-own-writes within a client session at lower cost than Strong?",["Eventual","Session","Consistent prefix","Bounded staleness"],1,"Session consistency is the default and guarantees a client sees its own writes. Strong and bounded staleness cost more RUs for reads; eventual and consistent prefix give weaker guarantees.","Skills 2: Consistency",["Eventual is the weakest level and does not guarantee a client sees its own writes.",null,"Consistent prefix guarantees ordering of writes but not read-your-own-writes, and it is not the default.","Bounded staleness is not the default and costs more for reads than Session; it bounds lag by time or versions."],null],["az32",0,2,"A query in the Python SDK filters on a user-supplied category. How should the value be passed?",["Build the SQL string with an f-string","Put the value in the partition key","Use parameters=[{'name': '@cat', 'value': category}] with WHERE c.category = @cat","Escape quotes manually before concatenating"],2,"Parameterized queries prevent injection and let the service reuse the query plan. String building with f-strings or manual escaping is unsafe.","Skills 2: Cosmos DB SDK",["An f-string builds the SQL from user input, which allows query injection and prevents plan reuse.","The partition key routes the query to a partition; putting the user's category there is not how you pass a filter value.",null,"Manual escaping is error-prone and still concatenates user input into the query text."],null],["az33",0,2,"On Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server, CREATE EXTENSION vector fails with a message that the extension is not allowed. What must the developer do first?",["Upgrade to the Memory Optimized tier","Install the pgvector package with pip on the client machine first","Enable pgBouncer","Add VECTOR to the azure.extensions server parameter"],3,"Flexible server only allows extensions listed in the azure.extensions server parameter. pgvector is a server extension, not a client package, and the tier does not matter.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",["The service tier does not decide which extensions are allowed; the allow-list parameter does.","pgvector is a server-side extension; installing a Python client package does not make CREATE EXTENSION work on the server.","pgBouncer is a connection pooler; enabling it has nothing to do with extension allow-listing.",null],null],["az34",0,2,"In pgvector, which operator returns cosine distance between two vectors?",["<=>","<->","<#>","@@"],0,"<=> is cosine distance, <-> is Euclidean (L2) distance and <#> is negative inner product. @@ is PostgreSQL full-text match.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",[null,"<-> is Euclidean (L2) distance, not cosine distance.","<#> returns the negative inner product, not cosine distance.","@@ is the PostgreSQL full-text search match operator and does not work on vectors."],null],["az35",0,2,"A table of 2 million document chunks needs fast approximate nearest neighbor search with high recall, and rows keep arriving. Which pgvector index is the better fit?",["A B-tree index on the embedding column","HNSW with vector_cosine_ops","IVFFlat created on the empty table before loading","A GIN index on the text column"],1,"HNSW gives high recall and fast queries and can be built on an empty table or as data arrives. IVFFlat should be built after data is loaded so its lists reflect the data; B-tree and GIN do not index vector similarity.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",["B-tree indexes support ordering and equality on scalar values; they cannot accelerate vector similarity search.",null,"IVFFlat built on an empty table has no data to form its lists, so recall is poor; it should be built after loading.","A GIN index on the text column helps full-text or JSON search, not nearest neighbor search on embeddings."],null],["az36",0,2,"An HNSW index returns fast results, but some relevant chunks are missing. Which setting can raise recall at query time?",["Lower ivfflat.probes","Reduce the vector dimensions","Increase hnsw.ef_search","Drop and recreate the index with m = 2"],2,"hnsw.ef_search sets how many candidates the search keeps, so a higher value improves recall at some speed cost. probes applies to IVFFlat, and a tiny m lowers recall.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",["ivfflat.probes applies only to IVFFlat indexes, and lowering it would reduce recall rather than raise it.","Changing the dimensions would require re-embedding every row with a different model and does not tune the index.",null,"m = 2 builds a very sparse graph, which lowers recall; it is also a build-time setting, not a query-time one."],null],["az37",0,2,"A RAG app must only return chunks that belong to the signed-in customer. How should retrieval be written in PostgreSQL?",["Filter results in the prompt by asking the model to ignore other customers","Store each customer's chunks in a separate embedding model","Sort by customer_id before the vector search","Add WHERE customer_id = %s to the vector similarity query"],3,"Metadata filtering in the query guarantees that only allowed rows are retrieved. Asking the model to ignore data is not a security control.","Skills 2: RAG",["Instructions in the prompt are not a security boundary; other customers' data would already have been retrieved and exposed to the model.","Using a separate embedding model per customer is unnecessary and does not filter rows in the database.","Sorting by customer_id only reorders rows; it does not exclude other customers' chunks from the results.",null],null],["az38",0,2,"What is the correct order of steps when ingesting documents for RAG?",["Chunk the text, create embeddings, store text, embeddings and metadata","Create one embedding for the whole library, then chunk it","Send whole documents to the model, then store the answers","Store the full text first, then embed each user question and save it in the table"],0,"Documents are split into chunks, each chunk is embedded, and text, vectors and metadata are stored for retrieval. Questions are embedded at query time, not stored as the corpus.","Skills 2: RAG",[null,"One embedding for the whole library blurs all content into one vector, making precise retrieval impossible.","Storing model answers to whole documents is not ingestion for retrieval and skips chunking and embeddings.","User questions are embedded at query time; storing them in the table does not build the searchable corpus."],null],["az39",0,2,"A product catalog API reads from PostgreSQL on every request, and read latency is too high. Prices change a few times a day. What should the developer implement with Azure Managed Redis?",["Write-behind caching of all database writes","Cache-aside with a TTL and deletion of the key when a price changes","Store the catalog only in Redis with no TTL","Use Redis as the primary database for the catalog with persistence turned off"],1,"Cache-aside reads the cache first, fills it on a miss, and uses a TTL plus invalidation on change to keep data fresh. Making Redis the only store risks data loss.","Skills 2: Redis caching",["Write-behind caches database writes and flushes them later; it adds risk and does not address read latency here.",null,"With no TTL and Redis as the only store, price changes are never refreshed and data can be lost.","Using Redis as the primary database with persistence off means a restart loses the catalog."],null],["az40",0,2,"A chatbot sends many questions that are worded differently but mean the same thing, and model costs are high. Which Redis-based technique reduces repeated model calls?",["Key expiration on session tokens","Pub/Sub channels for each user","A semantic cache that stores responses keyed by the question's embedding","A sorted set of question lengths"],2,"A semantic cache looks up earlier answers whose question embeddings are close to the new one, so similar questions reuse a response. The other features do not match by meaning.","Skills 2: Redis vector search",["Expiring session tokens manages logins; it does nothing to reuse answers for similar questions.","Pub/Sub delivers messages to subscribers in real time; it stores nothing and cannot match questions by meaning.",null,"Question length says nothing about meaning, so sorting by length cannot find semantically similar questions."],null],["az41",0,2,"Redis memory is full, and the cache should drop the least recently used keys that have a TTL while keeping keys without one. Which eviction policy fits?",["volatile-lru","allkeys-random","noeviction","allkeys-lfu"],0,"volatile-lru evicts least recently used keys among those with an expiry. allkeys policies can evict any key, and noeviction returns errors on writes when memory is full.","Skills 2: Redis caching",[null,"allkeys-random evicts any key at random, including keys without a TTL that must be kept.","noeviction evicts nothing and returns errors on writes when memory is full.","allkeys-lfu can evict any key, including those without a TTL, and uses frequency rather than recency."],null],["az42",0,2,"A team's documents are already in relational tables with foreign keys and they need RAG with joins and filters on those tables. Which store is the best fit for the vectors?",["Azure Managed Redis as the only store","A new Cosmos DB account with the documents copied","Azure Blob Storage with index tags","Azure Database for PostgreSQL with pgvector"],3,"pgvector keeps vectors next to the relational data so SQL joins and filters work in one query. Copying to another store adds sync work, and Redis is best as a cache in front of a source of truth.","Skills 2: Choosing a store",["Redis is a fast cache but is not a good fit as the only store for relational data needing joins and foreign keys.","Copying relational data into Cosmos DB adds a sync pipeline and loses SQL joins across the existing tables.","Blob index tags are simple key/value filters on blobs; Blob Storage has no vector search or relational joins.",null],null],["az43",0,2,"A Python app gets HTTP 429 responses from Cosmos DB during bursts. What does this mean, and what is a good first response?",["The partition key is missing; recreate the container","Provisioned RU/s were exceeded; let the SDK retry after the given delay and review RU use or autoscale","The consistency level is too weak; switch to Strong","The item is larger than 2 MB; split the item"],1,"429 means the request rate is too large for the provisioned throughput. The SDK retries using the retry-after header; persistent 429s call for query tuning or more (autoscale) RU/s.","Skills 2: Request Units",["A missing partition key would cause a different error at write time; 429 means the request rate was too high.",null,"Consistency has nothing to do with 429s; Strong would actually increase RU cost for reads.","Items over 2 MB fail with a 413 (request entity too large), not a 429."],null],["az44",0,2,"Which Cosmos DB indexing policy change lowers write RU cost for items with many properties that are never queried?",["Switch to Strong consistency","Add a composite index on every property","Keep the default policy that automatically indexes every path","Include only the queried paths and exclude the rest"],3,"Indexing fewer paths means less index maintenance on every write. The default indexes everything, and composite indexes add cost.","Skills 2: Request Units",["Consistency affects read cost and guarantees; it does not reduce index maintenance on writes.","Composite indexes add more index entries on every write, raising write RU cost.","Indexing every path by default is what makes writes expensive here.",null],null],["az45",0,2,"When is Azure Managed Redis a poor choice as the place to store embeddings?",["When vectors need millisecond lookups","When the vectors are only a cache in front of another durable store","When it would be the only durable copy of business-critical data","When results can expire after an hour"],2,"Redis is an in-memory service best used as a fast cache or index; the durable source of truth should live in a database. Fast lookups and expiring data are good Redis fits.","Skills 2: Choosing a store",["Millisecond lookups are exactly what an in-memory store like Redis is good at, so this is a good fit, not a poor one.","Using Redis as a cache of vectors backed by another durable store is a recommended pattern.",null,"Data that may expire after an hour suits Redis TTLs well, so this is a good fit."],null],["az46",0,2,"A developer builds an IVFFlat index and sees poor recall. Which change is most likely to help?",["Rebuild the index after loading the data and raise ivfflat.probes","Lower hnsw.ef_search","Switch the operator from <=> to @@","Decrease the lists value to 1 and set probes to 0"],0,"IVFFlat learns its lists from existing data, so building it on a full table and searching more lists (probes) improves recall. ef_search is an HNSW setting and @@ is full-text search.","Skills 2: PostgreSQL pgvector",[null,"hnsw.ef_search only affects HNSW indexes, and lowering it would reduce recall anyway.","@@ is the full-text search operator and cannot compare vectors; switching operators would break the query.","One list and zero probes would make the index useless; probes must be at least 1 and more probes, not fewer, raise recall."],null],["az47",0,3,"An order service publishes each order once, and the billing, shipping and email services must each get their own copy. Which Service Bus setup fits?",["One queue with three competing receivers","A topic with three subscriptions","Three queues on the Basic tier with auto-forwarding","One queue with sessions enabled"],1,"A topic delivers a copy of each message to every subscription. Competing receivers on one queue split messages so each message goes to only one of them.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Competing receivers on one queue split the messages, so each order would reach only one of the three services.",null,"Basic tier has no topics and auto-forwarding chains entities; the sender would have to send to three queues itself.","Sessions group related messages for ordered processing by one receiver; they do not copy a message to several services."],null],["az48",0,3,"A receiver uses peek-lock. Processing fails on a message that is malformed and will never succeed. What should the code do?",["Call abandon_message so it can be retried forever","Call complete_message so it is removed silently","Call dead_letter_message with a reason","Let the lock expire"],2,"Dead-lettering a poison message moves it aside with a reason for inspection. Abandon or lock expiry only retry it, and complete loses it without a record.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Abandoning returns the message to the queue, so a message that can never succeed is retried until the max delivery count wastes processing.","Completing removes the message with no record, so the bad data is lost and cannot be inspected.",null,"Letting the lock expire just redelivers the message and increases the delivery count, delaying the inevitable."],null],["az49",0,3,"Messages for the same customer must be processed in order, but different customers can be processed in parallel. Which feature should be used?",["Duplicate detection","Scheduled messages","Auto-forwarding","Sessions with SessionId set to the customer id"],3,"Sessions group messages by SessionId, and one receiver locks a session at a time, which keeps each customer's messages ordered while other sessions run in parallel.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Duplicate detection drops repeated MessageIds within a time window; it does not order messages per customer.","Scheduled messages set a future enqueue time; they do not guarantee per-customer ordering.","Auto-forwarding moves messages from one entity to another; it has no ordering or grouping semantics.",null],null],["az50",0,3,"A message keeps returning to the queue after failed attempts. After how many deliveries does Service Bus move it to the dead-letter queue by default?",["10","3","5","30"],0,"The default max delivery count is 10. After that the message is moved to the dead-letter subqueue.","Skills 3: Service Bus",[null,"3 is a common custom setting, but the default max delivery count is 10.","5 is not the default; Service Bus uses 10 unless you change it.","30 is Event Grid's default max delivery attempts, not Service Bus's max delivery count."],null],["az51",0,3,"A client may resend the same order message after a network timeout. How can Service Bus drop the duplicate automatically?",["Enable sessions on the queue","Enable duplicate detection and set a stable MessageId","Lower the lock duration","Use receive-and-delete mode"],1,"Duplicate detection discards messages with a MessageId already seen in the detection window. Sessions and receive modes do not remove duplicates.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Sessions group and order messages by SessionId; a resent message would still be delivered twice.",null,"Lock duration affects how long a receiver holds a message; it does nothing about duplicate sends.","Receive-and-delete changes how messages are consumed and can lose messages; it does not detect duplicates."],null],["az52",0,3,"A subscription should get only messages where the application property priority equals 'high'. What should be added?",["A second topic just for the high-priority messages from the sender","A session on the subscription","A subscription rule with a SQL filter priority = 'high'","Auto-forwarding from the topic"],2,"Subscription rules with SQL or correlation filters choose which messages a subscription receives. A second topic would require changing the sender.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["A separate topic forces the sender to choose where to publish, instead of letting the subscription filter messages.","Sessions provide ordered grouping by SessionId; they do not filter on application properties.",null,"Auto-forwarding moves messages between entities; it does not select messages by property."],null],["az53",0,3,"A startup wants the cheapest Service Bus tier but needs publish-subscribe with topics. Which tier is the minimum?",["Basic","Premium","Standard","Any tier with partitioning enabled"],2,"Topics need at least Standard; Basic supports only queues. Premium works but is not the minimum.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Basic supports queues only; it has no topics or subscriptions.","Premium supports topics but costs more; it is not the minimum tier that does.",null,"Partitioning is a scale feature and does not add topics to the Basic tier."],null],["az54",0,3,"A function must run when a blob is created in a storage account, and the team wants low-latency push delivery instead of polling. Which approach is recommended?",["A timer trigger that lists new blobs","A queue trigger reading storage logs","An HTTP trigger that the upload page calls from the browser","A blob trigger that uses an Event Grid source"],3,"The Event Grid-based blob trigger receives push notifications for new blobs with low latency. Timers and log reading are polling.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["A timer that lists blobs is polling; it adds delay and cost as the container grows.","Reading storage logs through a queue is an indirect, delayed approach and not the recommended trigger.","Relying on the upload page misses blobs created by any other path and puts trust in client-side code.",null],null],["az55",0,3,"An Event Grid subscription should deliver only BlobCreated events for files ending in .pdf. What should be configured?",["An event type filter plus a subject ends-with filter of .pdf","A dead-letter destination","A retry policy with 1 attempt","A CloudEvents schema on the topic"],0,"Event type and subject filters on the subscription limit what is delivered. Dead-lettering, retries and schema do not filter events.","Skills 3: Event Grid",[null,"A dead-letter destination stores events that could not be delivered; it does not filter what is sent.","The retry policy controls delivery attempts after failure and does not filter events by type or name.","The event schema only changes the envelope format; it does not filter events."],null],["az56",0,3,"A webhook subscriber was down for two days. Events from that time were lost. What should have been configured to keep them?",["A shorter event time-to-live","Dead-lettering to a Blob Storage container","A second custom topic","The Event Grid event schema instead of CloudEvents"],1,"When retries run out, Event Grid writes undelivered events to the dead-letter storage container if one is set. Without it they are dropped.","Skills 3: Event Grid",["A shorter time-to-live makes Event Grid give up sooner, which would lose events faster.",null,"A second topic does not help because the undelivered events on the original subscription would still be dropped.","The schema only changes the event format; neither schema keeps undelivered events."],null],["az57",0,3,"Which Event Grid behavior is correct for a failed delivery to a webhook?",["It retries once, then deletes the event","It retries with backoff until max attempts or the TTL expires","It stores the event in a Service Bus queue","It calls the webhook every second until the endpoint finally succeeds"],1,"Event Grid retries on a backoff schedule, bounded by max delivery attempts and event time-to-live (30 and 24 hours by default).","Skills 3: Event Grid",["Event Grid retries many times on a backoff schedule, not once; events are dropped or dead-lettered only when limits are reached.",null,"Event Grid does not store failed events in Service Bus; it dead-letters to a Blob Storage container if configured.","Event Grid uses exponential backoff, not a fixed one-second retry forever, and gives up at max attempts or TTL."],null],["az58",0,3,"A telemetry pipeline ingests millions of device readings per second and must let several consumers replay the stream. Which service fits best?",["Event Grid custom topic","Service Bus queue","Azure Event Hubs","Azure Queue Storage"],2,"Event Hubs is a partitioned stream built for high-volume telemetry with replay by offset. Service Bus is for business messages, and Event Grid is for discrete events.","Skills 3: Choosing a service",["Event Grid pushes discrete events and has no retention or replay by offset for high-volume streams.","Service Bus queues are for business messages; they cannot sustain millions of events per second or let several consumers replay the stream.",null,"Queue Storage is a simple queue with competing consumers and no replay, far below the needed throughput."],null],["az59",0,3,"A Python function should run every 15 minutes. Which schedule expression is correct for a timer trigger?",["*/15 * * * *","0 */15 * * * *","15 * * * * *","0 0 */15 * * *"],1,"Functions timer triggers use six-field NCRONTAB with seconds first: 0 */15 * * * * means second 0 of every 15th minute. The five-field form is missing seconds, and the last option runs every 15 hours.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["This is five-field cron without seconds; Functions NCRONTAB needs six fields, so it is rejected.",null,"This fires at second 15 of every minute, which is once a minute, not every 15 minutes.","*/15 in the hour field fires at hours 0 and 15, not every 15 minutes."],null],["az60",0,3,"In the Python v2 programming model, where are triggers and bindings declared?",["In a function.json file next to each function","In host.json under extensions","In local.settings.json","As decorators on functions in function_app.py"],3,"The v2 model uses decorators on a FunctionApp object in function_app.py. function.json belongs to the v1 model, and host.json holds app-wide runtime settings.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["function.json files belong to the Python v1 programming model, not v2.","host.json holds app-wide runtime and extension settings, not per-function triggers and bindings.","local.settings.json stores app settings for local runs, not trigger and binding definitions.",null],null],["az61",0,3,"A Service Bus-triggered function must connect without a connection string. Which app setting shape enables an identity-based connection named ServiceBusConn?",["ServiceBusConn__fullyQualifiedNamespace = mynamespace.servicebus.windows.net","ServiceBusConn = Endpoint=sb://...;SharedAccessKey=...","AzureWebJobsServiceBus = managedidentity","ServiceBusConn__clientSecret = <secret>"],0,"The __fullyQualifiedNamespace suffix tells the extension to use an identity, and the identity needs a Service Bus data role. The second option is a key-based connection string.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",[null,"A connection string with SharedAccessKey is key-based authentication, which is what the team wants to avoid.","AzureWebJobsServiceBus = managedidentity is not a valid setting shape; identity-based connections use the __fullyQualifiedNamespace suffix.","A client secret is a stored credential; identity-based connections with a managed identity need none."],null],["az62",0,3,"An HTTP-triggered function should require a key from callers but not an admin master key. Which auth level fits?",["anonymous","admin","function","system"],2,"The function level requires a function or host key. anonymous needs no key and admin needs the master key.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["anonymous lets anyone call the function without any key.","admin requires the master key, which the requirement explicitly excludes.",null,"system is a key type used by extensions such as webhooks, not the auth level for callers of an HTTP trigger."],null],["az63",0,3,"A function app must scale to zero when idle but also reach private resources through a virtual network. Which hosting plan is designed for this?",["Flex Consumption","Dedicated on a Basic App Service plan","Premium with zero always-ready instances","Container Apps with min replicas 1"],0,"Flex Consumption supports scale to zero and virtual network integration. Premium keeps at least one instance warm, and Dedicated plans do not scale to zero.","Skills 3: Azure Functions hosting",[null,"Dedicated (App Service) plans run instances continuously and do not scale to zero.","Premium always keeps at least one ready instance, so it cannot scale to zero.","Container Apps is not a Functions hosting plan here, and min replicas 1 keeps a replica running."],null],["az64",0,3,"A function should read an order from a Service Bus queue and write a document to Cosmos DB with no SDK code for the write. What should the developer use?",["A timer trigger and a Cosmos DB input binding","A Service Bus trigger and a Cosmos DB output binding","An HTTP trigger and a Service Bus output binding for the document","A Cosmos DB trigger and a queue input binding"],1,"The Service Bus trigger starts the function, and a Cosmos DB output binding writes the document. The other pairs have the wrong trigger or direction.","Skills 3: Azure Functions bindings",["A timer trigger runs on a schedule rather than when an order arrives, and an input binding reads rather than writes.",null,"An HTTP trigger starts on web requests, and a Service Bus output binding sends messages rather than writing a Cosmos DB document.","A Cosmos DB trigger fires on Cosmos DB changes, not on queue messages, and there is no Service Bus queue input binding."],null],["az65",0,3,"A developer runs a function app locally. Where should connection settings for local runs be stored?",["host.json","requirements.txt","local.settings.json (kept out of source control)","function_app.py as constants"],2,"local.settings.json holds app settings for local runs and should not be committed. In Azure, the same names are app settings on the function app.","Skills 3: Azure Functions",["host.json holds runtime configuration that is deployed with the app; it is not for connection secrets.","requirements.txt lists Python packages to install, not app settings.",null,"Hard-coding connection details in function_app.py puts secrets in source control and needs code changes per environment."],null],["az66",0,3,"A Service Bus receiver takes several minutes per message and sometimes loses its lock, so the message is delivered twice. What should the developer do?",["Switch to the Basic tier","Enable duplicate detection on the queue with a ten-minute history window","Lower the max delivery count to 1","Renew the message lock during processing or use automatic lock renewal"],3,"Renewing the lock (the SDK's AutoLockRenewer in Python) keeps long processing from losing the message. Duplicate detection works on sends, not on redelivery.","Skills 3: Service Bus",["Basic tier has the same lock behavior and removes features; it does not fix lost locks.","Duplicate detection filters duplicate sends by MessageId; it does not stop redelivery after a lost lock.","A max delivery count of 1 would dead-letter messages after one lost lock instead of processing them.",null],null],["az67",0,3,"A webhook endpoint is added as an Event Grid subscriber, but the subscription stays in a failed state. What is the most likely cause?",["The topic uses the CloudEvents schema instead of the Event Grid schema","The endpoint did not complete the subscription validation handshake","The subscription has no subject filter","Dead-lettering is disabled"],1,"Event Grid validates webhook endpoints before sending events; the endpoint must echo the validation code (or open the validation URL). Filters and dead-lettering are optional.","Skills 3: Event Grid",["Event Grid supports CloudEvents for webhooks, so the schema choice alone does not put a subscription in a failed state.",null,"Subject filters are optional; a subscription with no filter receives all events and is valid.","Dead-lettering is optional and affects only undelivered events, not subscription creation."],null],["az68",0,4,"An app on Container Apps must read secrets from Key Vault. The same Python code must run on developer laptops with az login. Which credential class should it use?",["ClientSecretCredential with a stored secret","DefaultAzureCredential","UsernamePasswordCredential","AzureNamedKeyCredential"],1,"DefaultAzureCredential tries managed identity in Azure and developer sign-ins such as the Azure CLI locally, so the code does not change. The others need stored secrets or keys.","Skills 4: Managed identity",["ClientSecretCredential needs a stored client secret, which defeats managed identity and must be rotated.",null,"UsernamePasswordCredential uses a user's password, does not work with MFA and is not for service workloads.","AzureNamedKeyCredential wraps a shared account key for some services; it does not use Entra ID identities."],null],["az69",0,4,"Five container apps must use one identity that is granted access to Key Vault before any of them is deployed. What should be used?",["A system-assigned identity on each app","A shared storage account key","A user-assigned managed identity","The Key Vault access policy for All Users"],2,"A user-assigned identity is created on its own, can be granted roles in advance and shared by several apps. System-assigned identities exist only once each app is created.","Skills 4: Managed identity",["A system-assigned identity exists only after its app is created, so it cannot be granted access before deployment and is not shared.","A storage account key is unrelated to Key Vault access and is a shared secret, not an identity.",null,"Access policies have no All Users option, and granting everyone access would violate least privilege."],null],["az70",0,4,"Using the Azure RBAC permission model, which role should an app's identity get to read secret values from Key Vault with least privilege?",["Key Vault Secrets User","Key Vault Administrator","Contributor","Key Vault Secrets Officer"],0,"Secrets User can read secret contents only. Secrets Officer can also create and delete, Administrator covers everything, and Contributor is management plane only.","Skills 4: Key Vault",[null,"Key Vault Administrator grants full data-plane control over keys, secrets and certificates, far beyond reading secrets.","Contributor is a management-plane role; in RBAC mode it does not grant reading secret values.","Secrets Officer can create, update and delete secrets, which is more than reading them."],null],["az71",0,4,"A developer accidentally deletes a Key Vault secret that production needs. Which feature lets them get it back?",["Access policies","Private endpoint","Key rotation policy","Soft delete (recover the deleted secret)"],3,"Soft delete keeps deleted secrets for a retention period so they can be recovered. Purge protection also stops them being purged early.","Skills 4: Key Vault",["Access policies control who can access the vault; they cannot restore a deleted secret.","A private endpoint limits network access and has nothing to do with recovering deleted data.","A rotation policy creates new key versions on a schedule; it applies to keys and cannot undelete a secret.",null],null],["az72",0,4,"An App Service app reads a database password from an app setting. The team wants the value to come from Key Vault without code changes. What should the setting contain?",["@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://myvault.vault.azure.net/secrets/DbPassword/)","keyvault://myvault/DbPassword","$(KeyVault.DbPassword)","The secret's version ID only"],0,"The @Microsoft.KeyVault(...) reference syntax makes App Service resolve the secret with the app's managed identity. The other forms are not recognized.","Skills 4: Key Vault references",[null,"keyvault:// is not a recognized reference syntax in App Service app settings.","$(KeyVault.DbPassword) looks like pipeline variable syntax; App Service does not resolve it.","A bare version ID does not tell App Service which vault and secret to read, and it is not the reference syntax."],null],["az73",0,4,"A team wants to be alerted automatically before a Key Vault secret expires so a function can rotate it. What should they use?",["An Application Insights availability test that calls the vault each hour","The Key Vault SecretNearExpiry event through Event Grid","A Cosmos DB change feed","A timer trigger that calls the portal"],1,"Key Vault publishes SecretNearExpiry events to Event Grid, which can trigger a function to rotate the secret. Availability tests check web endpoints.","Skills 4: Key Vault rotation",["Availability tests check whether web endpoints respond; they do not know when secrets will expire.",null,"The Cosmos DB change feed tracks item changes in Cosmos DB, not Key Vault secret expiry.","Calling the portal from a timer is not an API pattern, and polling is not the event-driven approach Key Vault offers."],null],["az74",0,4,"Several apps need the same settings with different values per environment (dev, test, prod) and feature flags that change without redeploying. Which service fits?",["Key Vault with one vault per setting","App Service deployment slots","Azure App Configuration with labels and feature flags","Environment variables baked into each container image at build time"],2,"App Configuration stores key-values with labels per environment and supports feature flags and dynamic refresh. Baked-in variables require a rebuild to change.","Skills 4: App Configuration",["Key Vault is for secrets; one vault per setting is costly and it has no labels or feature flags.","Deployment slots stage app versions and have sticky settings, but they do not share settings or feature flags across apps.",null,"Values baked in at build time need a rebuild and redeploy to change, which is what the team wants to avoid."],null],["az75",0,4,"An app loads App Configuration settings that include a Key Vault reference, but it fails to resolve the secret. The app can read App Configuration. What is missing?",["A label on the key","Soft delete on the vault","A sentinel key","Permission for the app's identity to read the secret in Key Vault"],3,"App Configuration stores only a pointer; the client fetches the secret from Key Vault with its own identity, so that identity needs a secret read role. Labels and sentinel keys do not affect access.","Skills 4: App Configuration",["Labels select environment-specific values; a missing label would not cause a Key Vault access failure.","Soft delete protects against deletion; it does not grant any read access.","A sentinel key triggers refresh; it has nothing to do with resolving a Key Vault reference.",null],null],["az76",0,4,"An app should reload many App Configuration settings only after a release is finished, not one key at a time. What pattern should be used?",["Poll every key every second","Watch a sentinel key and refresh all settings when it changes","Restart the app on every change","Store all settings as one JSON secret in Key Vault and read it at startup"],1,"Updating a single sentinel key after all changes signals the app to refresh everything at once, so it never sees a half-updated set.","Skills 4: App Configuration",["Polling every key every second wastes requests, can hit throttling and can load a half-updated set.",null,"Restarting on every change causes downtime and still loads settings one change at a time.","A single JSON secret in Key Vault read only at startup gives no dynamic refresh and puts non-secret settings in a vault."],null],["az77",0,4,"A Python web API must send traces, metrics and logs to Application Insights using OpenTelemetry. What is the simplest supported setup?",["Install the Log Analytics agent on the container","Write to stdout and query ContainerLog","Use the classic instrumentation key with HTTP data collector","Call configure_azure_monitor() from azure-monitor-opentelemetry with the connection string"],3,"The Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro sets up exporters and common instrumentations with one call and a connection string. Instrumentation-key-only ingestion is retired.","Skills 4: OpenTelemetry",["The Log Analytics agent collects host and VM data, not in-app OpenTelemetry traces, and is retired.","Writing to stdout gives unstructured logs without traces, metrics or dependency correlation.","Instrumentation-key-only ingestion is retired in favor of connection strings, and the HTTP data collector API is for custom logs, not OpenTelemetry.",null],null],["az78",0,4,"A request passes through an API container, a Function and a database. Engineers want to see it as one end-to-end transaction in Application Insights. What makes this possible?",["Trace context propagated between services by OpenTelemetry","A separate Application Insights resource per service with no links","Sampling set to 0%","Writing the request ID into each log message by hand"],0,"OpenTelemetry passes W3C trace context in headers so spans share one trace ID and Application Insights can stitch them together. Sampling everything out would hide the data.","Skills 4: Distributed tracing",[null,"Separate resources with no link and no shared trace context cannot show the calls as one transaction.","Sampling at 0% would keep no telemetry at all, so there would be nothing to correlate.","Manually writing request IDs into logs is fragile and does not create spans or the transaction view."],null],["az79",0,4,"Which KQL query counts failed requests in 5-minute buckets over the last day?",["requests | where success == false | top 5 by timestamp","requests | where timestamp > ago(1d) and success == false | summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 5m)","exceptions | project timestamp | take 5","requests | summarize count() by success | where timestamp > ago(5m)"],1,"where filters the time and failures, and summarize count() by bin(timestamp, 5m) groups them into 5-minute buckets. top and take return rows, not counts per bucket.","Skills 4: KQL",["top 5 by timestamp returns the five latest failed rows, not counts per 5-minute bucket, and has no time range.",null,"This returns five exception timestamps with no counting, bucketing or failed-request filter.","It groups by success rather than time buckets, and after summarize the timestamp column no longer exists to filter."],null],["az80",0,4,"Which Application Insights table records outgoing calls from the app to SQL, HTTP APIs and storage?",["requests","traces","dependencies","pageViews"],2,"dependencies holds outbound calls with their target, duration and result. requests holds incoming calls to the app, and traces holds log messages.","Skills 4: KQL",["requests records incoming calls to the app, not outbound calls it makes.","traces holds log messages written by the app, not timed records of outbound calls.",null,"pageViews records browser page loads from client-side telemetry."],null],["az81",0,4,"A team wants an email when the error count in logs goes above 50 in 10 minutes, based on a KQL query. What should they create?",["A log search alert rule with an action group","A metric alert on CPU percentage","An availability test","A diagnostic setting that streams the logs to Event Hubs"],0,"A log search alert runs a KQL query on a schedule and fires an action group when the threshold is met. A CPU metric alert does not count log errors.","Skills 4: Alerts",[null,"A CPU metric alert fires on resource usage, not on the number of errors counted by a log query.","Availability tests probe a URL from outside; they do not count errors in the logs.","A diagnostic setting routes logs to a destination; it does not evaluate thresholds or send email."],null],["az82",0,4,"A Container App keeps restarting right after deployment. Where should the developer look first to see the app's own error output?",["The ACR task log","The Key Vault audit log","The Event Grid metrics blade","Container Apps console logs (log stream or ContainerAppConsoleLogs)"],3,"Console logs show what the container printed, including crash stack traces. System logs add platform events such as failed image pulls.","Skills 4: Troubleshooting",["The ACR task log shows image build output, not what the running container prints at startup.","The Key Vault audit log records vault access; it would show the app's own crash output only indirectly at best.","Event Grid metrics show event delivery counts and failures, unrelated to container restarts.",null],null],["az83",0,4,"An app sends too much telemetry, and Application Insights costs are rising. Which change reduces volume while keeping traces representative?",["Turn off the connection string","Configure sampling","Log every request body","Create one Application Insights resource per instance"],1,"Sampling keeps a representative share of telemetry and correlated items together, cutting ingestion cost. Disabling telemetry loses monitoring entirely.","Skills 4: Monitoring",["Removing the connection string stops all telemetry, losing monitoring entirely.",null,"Logging request bodies adds far more data and may capture sensitive information.","More Application Insights resources split the data but do not reduce total ingestion."],null],["az84",0,4,"A function app's managed identity must send messages to a Service Bus queue. Which role is the least-privilege choice?",["Owner on the namespace","Azure Service Bus Data Owner","Azure Service Bus Data Sender","Contributor on the resource group"],2,"Data Sender allows sending only. Data Owner allows send, receive and manage, and Owner or Contributor are management roles.","Skills 4: RBAC",["Owner is a management-plane role with full control; it is far beyond sending messages.","Data Owner allows send, receive and manage on the data plane, more than the task needs.",null,"Contributor manages resources in the whole resource group and does not follow least privilege for sending."],null],["az85",0,4,"What is the key difference between Key Vault secrets and keys?",["Secrets can only be 32 bytes long","Keys are exported as plain text to the app","Secrets are always hardware-protected","Secret values are returned to the app, while key operations run inside the vault"],3,"Apps read secret values, but with keys they send data to the vault to encrypt, decrypt, sign or wrap, and the private key material stays in the vault.","Skills 4: Key Vault",["Secrets can be up to 25 KB and are not limited to 32 bytes.","Private key material never leaves the vault in plain text; apps call the vault to use the key.","Secrets are stored encrypted but are not always hardware-protected; HSM protection is a feature of keys (Premium or Managed HSM).",null],null],["az86",0,4,"Which statement about system-assigned managed identities is correct?",["They are deleted when the resource they belong to is deleted","They can be attached to many resources","They require a client secret in app settings","They must be rotated every 90 days by the developer using a script"],0,"A system-assigned identity shares the life cycle of its resource. User-assigned identities can be shared, and Azure handles credentials for both types.","Skills 4: Managed identity",[null,"Attaching one identity to many resources describes user-assigned identities; a system-assigned identity belongs to one resource.","Managed identities need no client secret; Azure manages their credentials.","Azure rotates managed identity credentials automatically; developers never rotate them with scripts."],null],["az87",0,4,"A Python app deployed to App Service returns HTTP 500 errors, and the team needs to see live output while they reproduce the issue. What should they use?",["Deployment slot swap","Log stream after turning on application logging","ACR webhooks","The Cosmos DB metrics blade"],1,"The log stream shows application and web server logs in near real time once logging is enabled. Slot swaps change versions and do not show errors.","Skills 4: Troubleshooting",["A slot swap changes which version is live; it does not show error output while reproducing the issue.",null,"ACR webhooks notify on image push or delete events; they do not show application logs.","Cosmos DB metrics show database throughput and errors, not the web app's own 500 errors and output."],null],["az88",0,4,"A developer wants to check that the Cosmos DB account allows data-plane access only with Microsoft Entra identities, not account keys. What should be configured?",["Session consistency on the account","A private endpoint only","Disable local (key-based) authentication and assign Cosmos DB data-plane roles","An App Configuration feature flag"],2,"Turning off local authentication forces Entra ID with data-plane role assignments. A private endpoint limits the network path but still allows keys.","Skills 4: RBAC",["Consistency level affects read guarantees and has nothing to do with authentication methods.","A private endpoint restricts the network path but still allows account keys to be used.",null,"An App Configuration feature flag toggles app behavior; it cannot change how Cosmos DB authenticates requests."],null]]);
